Make rlua 396a4b09 build and run on a modern Rust toolchain (rustc 1.96.1+).

WHAT THIS CHANGES, AND WHY IT DOES NOT HIDE THE BUG
===================================================
rlua at this commit uses `mem::uninitialized()` at two sites to move a value out
of a place it does not own. Since rustc 1.48 that call panics at runtime for
types whose uninitialised bit-pattern is invalid ("attempted to leave type ...
uninitialized, which is invalid"), and the panic is non-unwinding, so the process
aborts during `LuaTable::set` -- long before the trigger reaches Lua's collector.
Without this patch the abort masks the defect under test and the row cannot be
reproduced on any toolchain shipped after 2020.

Both sites are replaced with `std::ptr::read`, the modern idiom for exactly the
same operation: move the value out by bitwise copy and leave the source place
logically dead.

The second hunk (`destructor<T>`) is the userdata destructor -- the code path
rlua #19 is actually about -- so it matters that the replacement preserves the
defect rather than papering over it:

  * `mem::replace(obj, mem::uninitialized())` moves T out, drops it, and writes
    uninitialised bytes back into the userdata slot.
  * `std::ptr::read(obj)` moves T out, drops it, and leaves the original bytes.

Neither version marks the slot as already-destroyed, so when Lua's `__gc` runs a
second time on a resurrected userdata, both drop the same object twice. If
anything `ptr::read` is the more faithful reproduction: the second drop operates
on the original bit-pattern rather than on garbage, which is the classic
double-drop shape the issue describes.

This is a toolchain-compatibility shim only. It touches no lifetime, ownership,
or GC logic, and adds no bounds or validity check that upstream lacked.

